{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And they accepted.</b> Like the Aramaic usage. It comes in singular form, meaning each and every individual accepted the daughters of his step. Or the word כל is missing, and the first interpretation is correct. One who is precise would say: וקבל reverts to its standard pattern [singular], yet it is read [as plural]. If so, what is the meaning of \"that which they had begun to do\"? Rather, it is that they accepted what they had done at first, for they did [it] in the following year [as] Purim." "<b>And what Mordecai wrote.</b> To do thus every year."], :book :esther, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {0 "The phrase 'בנות צעדה' is obscure; translated hyper-literally as 'daughters of his step.' Possibly a corruption or idiom of uncertain meaning. The argument about singular/plural form and the transition from 'began to do' to the following year is compressed and difficult."}, :source-witnesses [["Kol Sason, Krotoschin, 1840" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001728315"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:30:25.232035965Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}